/* Generated by tools/build-mock-bank.mjs. Do not edit. */
CT.mockBank = [{"id":"basics-0","topic":"basics","q":"Pointer vs reference?","a":"<div><p>A pointer holds an address, can be null, can be re-pointed, and needs <code>*</code> to use. A reference is a permanent alias that must be initialized and cannot be null. Use references for parameters, pointers when \"nothing\" is a valid value or you need to re-point.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-1","topic":"basics","q":"Stack vs heap?","a":"<div><p>The stack holds locals and is freed automatically when the scope ends; it is fast but small. The heap holds <code>new</code>-allocated objects that live until you free them; it is large but slower and you must manage the lifetime (or let a smart pointer do it).</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-2","topic":"basics","q":"What is a dangling pointer?","a":"<div><p>A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior. Setting a pointer to <code>nullptr</code> after <code>delete</code> helps, and smart pointers avoid the problem.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-3","topic":"basics","q":"<code>const int*</code> vs <code>int* const</code>?","a":"<div><p>Read right to left. <code>const int*</code> is a pointer to a const int (data is read-only, pointer can move). <code>int* const</code> is a const pointer to an int (pointer is fixed, data can change).</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-4","topic":"basics","q":"<code>delete</code> vs <code>delete[]</code>?","a":"<div><p>Memory from <code>new</code> must be freed with <code>delete</code>; memory from <code>new[]</code> must be freed with <code>delete[]</code> so every element's destructor runs. Mixing them is undefined behavior. Deleting <code>nullptr</code> is safe and does nothing.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-5","topic":"basics","q":"What is undefined behavior?","a":"<div><p>Code the standard places no requirements on: the compiler may assume it never happens, so anything can occur (crash, wrong output, or apparently fine). Examples: out-of-bounds access, null dereference, signed overflow, reading uninitialized memory, data races.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-6","topic":"basics","q":"How do you pass a large object to a function efficiently?","a":"<div><p>By <code>const</code> reference if the function only reads it, to avoid a copy. By non-const reference if it must modify the caller's object.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-0","topic":"oop","q":"Why must a base class destructor be virtual?","a":"<div><p>If you <code>delete</code> a derived object through a base pointer and the destructor is not virtual, only the base destructor runs: undefined behavior, and the derived part's resources leak. A virtual destructor makes <code>delete</code> dispatch to the most derived destructor first. Any class meant to be a polymorphic base needs one.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-1","topic":"oop","q":"What is the difference between overloading and overriding?","a":"<div><p>Overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameters in the same scope, chosen at compile time. Overriding: a derived class provides its own implementation of a base <i>virtual</i> function with the same signature, chosen at runtime.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-2","topic":"oop","q":"What is a pure virtual function and an abstract class?","a":"<div><p>A pure virtual function is declared <code>= 0</code> and has no required implementation. A class with at least one cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface that derived classes must implement.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-3","topic":"oop","q":"What is the Rule of Three / Five / Zero?","a":"<div><p>Three: if you define a destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, define all three. Five: add the move constructor and move assignment. Zero: design classes so none are needed by holding resources in self-managing members like <code>vector</code> and <code>unique_ptr</code>. Remember that declaring a destructor or a copy operation suppresses the move operations, so moves silently become copies.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-4","topic":"oop","q":"What is object slicing?","a":"<div><p>Copying a derived object into a base-class value discards the derived members and virtual behaviour. Avoid it by passing polymorphic objects by reference or pointer.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-5","topic":"oop","q":"What does <code>explicit</code> do?","a":"<div><p>It stops a constructor (or conversion operator) from being used for implicit conversions. Without it, <code>void f(Account); f(5);</code> could silently build an <code>Account</code> from 5.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-6","topic":"oop","q":"Can a constructor be virtual? Can a destructor be pure virtual?","a":"<div><p>A constructor cannot be virtual: the vptr is set up by the constructor, so there is no dynamic type yet. A destructor <i>can</i> be pure virtual to make a class abstract, but it still needs a body because derived destructors call it.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-7","topic":"oop","q":"Composition or inheritance?","a":"<div><p>Prefer composition. Use public inheritance only for a true is-a relationship where you need runtime polymorphism, and make sure it satisfies Liskov substitution.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-0","topic":"core","q":"What does <code>std::move</code> actually do?","a":"<div><p>Nothing at runtime: it is an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. It enables the move constructor or move assignment to be chosen. The moved-from object stays valid but unspecified. On a <code>const</code> object it silently copies, because a <code>const</code> rvalue cannot bind to a move constructor.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-1","topic":"core","q":"<code>unique_ptr</code> vs <code>shared_ptr</code> vs <code>weak_ptr</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>unique_ptr</code>: sole owner, move-only, zero overhead. <code>shared_ptr</code>: reference-counted shared ownership with a control block and atomic count. <code>weak_ptr</code>: non-owning observer used to break cycles and to check whether a shared object is still alive (<code>lock()</code>).</p></div>"},{"id":"core-2","topic":"core","q":"Is <code>shared_ptr</code> thread-safe?","a":"<div><p>The reference count is atomic, so copying and destroying <code>shared_ptr</code>s from different threads is safe. The pointed-to object is <b>not</b> protected: concurrent access to it still needs synchronization. Concurrently modifying the same <code>shared_ptr</code> variable is also a race.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-3","topic":"core","q":"What is copy elision / RVO?","a":"<div><p>The compiler constructs a returned object directly in the caller's storage, skipping the copy or move. In C++17 it is guaranteed when returning a temporary (prvalue). Do not write <code>return std::move(local)</code>: it prevents it.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-4","topic":"core","q":"What is stack unwinding?","a":"<div><p>When an exception propagates, the runtime exits scopes up the call stack, calling the destructors of all fully constructed locals until a handler is found. It is what makes RAII exception-safe.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-5","topic":"core","q":"Why should destructors not throw?","a":"<div><p>Destructors run during unwinding. If one throws while another exception is active, <code>std::terminate</code> is called. Destructors are <code>noexcept</code> by default; catch and handle errors inside them.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-6","topic":"core","q":"When would you use <code>dynamic_cast</code>?","a":"<div><p>For a checked downcast from a polymorphic base to a derived type when you cannot express the need through a virtual function. It returns null (pointers) or throws <code>bad_cast</code> (references) on failure. Frequent use suggests a design problem.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-7","topic":"core","q":"Difference between <code>const</code> and <code>constexpr</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>const</code> means read-only after initialization (the value may be determined at runtime). <code>constexpr</code> means the value or function can be evaluated at compile time and usable in constant expressions.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-0","topic":"stl","q":"How does <code>std::vector</code> grow?","a":"<div><p>When <code>size == capacity</code> it allocates a larger buffer (geometric growth, typically 1.5x to 2x, implementation-defined), moves or copies the elements, and frees the old buffer. This makes <code>push_back</code> amortised O(1), but it invalidates all iterators, pointers and references. <code>reserve</code> avoids the reallocations.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-1","topic":"stl","q":"<code>map</code> vs <code>unordered_map</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>map</code>: balanced tree, sorted iteration, O(log n), needs <code>operator&lt;</code>, supports range queries. <code>unordered_map</code>: hash table, average O(1) (worst O(n) with many collisions), no order, needs a hash and <code>==</code>. Choose <code>unordered_map</code> for plain lookups and <code>map</code> when you need order.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-2","topic":"stl","q":"<code>push_back</code> vs <code>emplace_back</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>push_back</code> takes an existing object (a copy or move). <code>emplace_back</code> forwards its arguments to the element's constructor and builds it in place, avoiding a temporary. For simple types there is no difference; for objects with expensive construction <code>emplace_back</code> can save a move.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-3","topic":"stl","q":"What is the erase-remove idiom?","a":"<div><p><code>std::remove</code>/<code>remove_if</code> only moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new end; it cannot shrink the container. Pass that to the container's <code>erase</code> to actually delete the tail: <code>v.erase(std::remove_if(...), v.end())</code>. C++20 has <code>std::erase_if</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-4","topic":"stl","q":"What is a hash collision and how is it handled?","a":"<div><p>Two different keys hash to the same bucket. The standard library's unordered containers resolve it with chaining (a bucket holds a list of entries), so lookups in a heavily colliding bucket degrade toward O(n). A good hash function and a sensible load factor (<code>rehash</code>, <code>reserve</code>) keep it near O(1).</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-5","topic":"stl","q":"Why can't I <code>std::sort</code> a <code>std::list</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>sort</code> needs random-access iterators (it jumps around by index). A list only provides bidirectional iterators, so it has its own member function <code>list::sort</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-6","topic":"stl","q":"Which container for a task queue? For \"top K\" elements?","a":"<div><p>A queue (<code>std::queue</code>, backed by <code>deque</code>) for FIFO tasks. A <code>priority_queue</code> (heap) for top K: keep a heap of size K and compare against its root: O(n log K).</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-0","topic":"templates","q":"What happens when you use a function template?","a":"<div><p>The compiler deduces the template arguments from the call, then <b>instantiates</b> a concrete function for that combination of types (once per distinct combination across the program), and compiles it like normal code. The template itself generates no code.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-1","topic":"templates","q":"What is a forwarding reference?","a":"<div><p>A <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> parameter where <code>T</code> is deduced for that very function template (or <code>auto&amp;&amp;</code>). It binds to lvalues and rvalues alike: for an lvalue <code>T</code> becomes <code>X&amp;</code> and reference collapsing makes the parameter <code>X&amp;</code>; for an rvalue <code>T</code> is <code>X</code> and the parameter is <code>X&amp;&amp;</code>. <code>std::forward</code> then preserves that category.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-2","topic":"templates","q":"What is perfect forwarding, and why do we need it?","a":"<div><p>Passing arguments through a wrapper to another function without changing their value category or constness, so the callee can still choose copy vs move correctly. Without it you would need an overload per combination of lvalue/rvalue/const for every parameter.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-3","topic":"templates","q":"Explain SFINAE.","a":"<div><p>If substituting deduced types into a function template's signature yields an invalid type or expression, that candidate is removed from the overload set rather than raising an error. <code>std::enable_if</code> exploits it to enable a function only for suitable types. C++17 <code>if constexpr</code> and C++20 concepts are the clearer modern tools.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-4","topic":"templates","q":"Full vs partial specialization?","a":"<div><p>Full: all template arguments fixed. Partial: a pattern with some parameters still free (<code>Foo&lt;T*&gt;</code>), allowed for class templates and variable templates but not for function templates (use overloading).</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-5","topic":"templates","q":"Why must template definitions be in headers?","a":"<div><p>The compiler needs the body to instantiate it in each translation unit that uses a particular type. If only the declaration is visible, no instantiation happens and the linker cannot find the function. Alternative: explicit instantiation in one <code>.cpp</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-6","topic":"templates","q":"What is a variadic template and how do you process the pack?","a":"<div><p>A template taking zero or more arguments via a parameter pack (<code>typename... Args</code>). Expand with <code>args...</code>. Process by recursion with a base case, or in C++17 with a fold expression like <code>(args + ...)</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-7","topic":"templates","q":"Templates vs inheritance for polymorphism?","a":"<div><p>Templates give static (compile-time) polymorphism: no runtime cost and inlinable, but the type must be known when compiling and each type duplicates code. Inheritance with virtual functions gives dynamic (runtime) polymorphism: one code path and heterogeneous collections, at the cost of an indirection and heap-allocated objects.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-0","topic":"patterns","q":"How do you make a Singleton thread-safe in C++?","a":"<div><p>Use a function-local static (Meyers Singleton). Since C++11 its initialization is guaranteed thread-safe and happens exactly once. Alternatives are <code>std::call_once</code> with an <code>std::once_flag</code>, or creating it before threads start. Hand-written double-checked locking is easy to get wrong. Remember the object's own methods still need locking if they mutate shared state.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-1","topic":"patterns","q":"What is wrong with Singleton?","a":"<div><p>It is global mutable state with a nice name: hidden dependencies, tight coupling, hard to test or mock, awkward lifetime and destruction order, and a problem for multithreading. Prefer passing a reference (dependency injection) unless a single shared instance is truly inherent.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-2","topic":"patterns","q":"Strategy vs State?","a":"<div><p>Both delegate behaviour to a swappable object. Strategy: the client selects an interchangeable algorithm and strategies are independent. State: the context changes its behaviour as its own state changes, and transitions are driven by the states or context.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-3","topic":"patterns","q":"What problems can Observer cause?","a":"<div><p>Dangling observers (destroyed but still registered), modification of the observer list during notification, unexpected notification order, cascading updates and performance issues, and thread-safety issues. Fixes: RAII detach, <code>weak_ptr</code>, iterate over a copy, and do not hold locks while calling out.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-4","topic":"patterns","q":"Why return <code>unique_ptr</code> from a factory?","a":"<div><p>It expresses that the caller takes sole ownership, prevents leaks, and the caller can convert it to a <code>shared_ptr</code> when sharing is needed. Returning a raw owning pointer is ambiguous and error-prone.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-5","topic":"patterns","q":"How do you replace a virtual-function Strategy in modern C++?","a":"<div><p>With a callable: store a <code>std::function</code> (runtime flexibility) or take a template parameter (zero overhead, compile-time choice). A lambda becomes the strategy, and no class hierarchy is needed.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-6","topic":"patterns","q":"What is the Open/Closed principle?","a":"<div><p>Software entities should be open to extension but closed to modification: you add new behaviour (a new strategy, a new shape type registered in a factory) without editing the existing, tested code.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-0","topic":"threads","q":"What is the difference between a process and a thread?","a":"<div><p>A process has its own address space; threads of one process share memory but each has its own stack and registers. Thread creation and switching are cheaper, communication is easy (shared memory) but needs synchronization.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-1","topic":"threads","q":"What is a data race? Is it the same as a race condition?","a":"<div><p>A data race is unsynchronised concurrent access to the same memory with at least one write: undefined behavior in C++. A race condition is the wider class of bugs where correctness depends on timing, which can exist even without a data race (two correctly locked steps that needed to be one atomic operation).</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-2","topic":"threads","q":"What happens if a <code>std::thread</code> is destroyed while still joinable, and why?","a":"<div><p><code>std::terminate</code> is called. The alternatives are worse: an implicit <code>join</code> can hide long waits, and an implicit <code>detach</code> leaves a running thread using locals that may be gone (undefined behavior). So make the thread unjoinable on every path: join in an RAII guard, or use <code>std::jthread</code> (C++20), which joins automatically.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-3","topic":"threads","q":"<code>lock_guard</code> vs <code>unique_lock</code> vs <code>scoped_lock</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>lock_guard</code>: simplest RAII lock for a scope. <code>unique_lock</code>: flexible (unlock/relock, deferred, movable) and needed by condition variables. <code>scoped_lock</code> (C++17): locks multiple mutexes at once with deadlock avoidance.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-4","topic":"threads","q":"What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it?","a":"<div><p>Threads wait for each other's locks forever. Needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevent with a consistent global lock order, <code>std::scoped_lock</code>/<code>std::lock</code> for multiple mutexes, short critical sections, no calls to foreign code under a lock, or <code>try_lock</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-5","topic":"threads","q":"Why does <code>condition_variable::wait</code> need a predicate / a loop?","a":"<div><p>Spurious wakeups (a thread may wake without a notification) and because the state may change again between the notification and the thread reacquiring the lock. The predicate re-checks the real condition.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-6","topic":"threads","q":"Why must the shared condition be modified under the mutex?","a":"<div><p>Otherwise the waiter could check the condition (false), then the notifier changes it and notifies, then the waiter goes to sleep: a lost wakeup. The mutex makes check-and-sleep atomic with respect to the update.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-7","topic":"threads","q":"<code>std::atomic</code> vs mutex?","a":"<div><p>An atomic makes operations on a single variable indivisible, usually lock-free and cheap. A mutex protects any critical section, including invariants over several variables, but may block. Use atomics for counters and flags, mutexes for compound state.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-8","topic":"threads","q":"Is <code>volatile</code> a substitute for <code>atomic</code>?","a":"<div><p>No. <code>volatile</code> prevents some compiler optimisations but gives no atomicity and no inter-thread ordering. Using it for synchronization is a data race.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-9","topic":"threads","q":"What are the memory orders?","a":"<div><p><code>seq_cst</code> (default, strongest, one global order), <code>acquire</code>/<code>release</code> (pair a release store with an acquire load to publish data), <code>relaxed</code> (atomicity only). Start with the default; weaken only with a reason and a proof.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-10","topic":"threads","q":"<code>std::async</code> vs <code>std::thread</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>async</code> returns a <code>future</code> carrying the result and any exception, and may use a pool or defer execution (use <code>launch::async</code> to force a thread). <code>thread</code> gives raw control with no result channel. A future from <code>async</code> blocks in its destructor.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-11","topic":"threads","q":"What is false sharing?","a":"<div><p>Threads writing different variables on the same cache line force the line to bounce between cores, slowing everything. Fix by padding or aligning per-thread data to the cache line size (<code>alignas(64)</code>).</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-12","topic":"threads","q":"Why avoid locks and heap allocation on a real-time hot path?","a":"<div><p>Both can block or take unpredictable time (contention, priority inversion, allocator locks). Use preallocated buffers and pools, per-thread data and lock-free structures such as an SPSC ring buffer to keep latency bounded.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-13","topic":"threads","q":"Is a function-local <code>static</code> initialised thread-safely?","a":"<div><p>Yes, since C++11 initialization happens exactly once even with concurrent callers (\"magic statics\"). That is why the Meyers Singleton is thread-safe. Using the object afterwards still needs its own synchronization.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-14","topic":"threads","q":"My class locks a mutex in every method. Is it thread-safe?","a":"<div><p>Not necessarily. Each call is safe on its own, but a caller's <i>sequence</i> of calls is not atomic. The classic case is a shared stack: <code>if (!s.empty()) { v = s.top(); s.pop(); }</code> races, because another thread can pop between the check and the use. The cause is the interface (it is also true of a lock-free stack), so the fix is to provide whole operations such as <code>std::optional&lt;T&gt; try_pop()</code> that check and remove under one lock.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-15","topic":"threads","q":"Can threads deadlock without any mutex? What are the practical rules against deadlock?","a":"<div><p>Yes: two threads that <code>join()</code> each other, or any cycle of waits on futures or condition variables. The rules all mean \"do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you\": avoid nested locks (or take several with <code>scoped_lock</code>), never call user-supplied code or leak references to protected data while holding a lock, lock in a fixed order, and use a lock hierarchy so a wrong order is caught immediately at runtime.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-16","topic":"threads","q":"How would you find a data race in a large program?","a":"<div><p>Build and run the tests with ThreadSanitizer (<code>-fsanitize=thread</code>), and Helgrind for lock-order problems; review shared state and make sure every access has a lock or is atomic; reduce sharing by design.</p></div>"},{"id":"strings-0","topic":"strings","q":"When is a <code>string_view</code> dangerous?","a":"When it outlives the string it views: views of temporaries, of locals returned from a function, or of a string that is later modified or reallocated."},{"id":"strings-1","topic":"strings","q":"<code>regex_match</code> versus <code>regex_search</code>?","a":"<code>regex_match</code> must match the whole input. <code>regex_search</code> finds a match anywhere inside it."},{"id":"strings-2","topic":"strings","q":"How do you iterate over every match?","a":"<code>std::sregex_iterator</code> (or <code>std::cregex_iterator</code> for C strings) over the range, compared with a default-constructed end iterator."},{"id":"strings-3","topic":"strings","q":"Which clock for a timeout, and why?","a":"<code>steady_clock</code>, because it is monotonic and unaffected by changes to the system time."},{"id":"strings-4","topic":"strings","q":"Why can <code>seconds = milliseconds</code> not compile but <code>milliseconds = seconds</code> can?","a":"Converting to a coarser unit would truncate, so it requires an explicit <code>duration_cast</code>. Converting to a finer unit is lossless, so it is implicit."},{"id":"strings-5","topic":"strings","q":"Output: what does this print? <code>std::string s = \"abc\"; std::cout &lt;&lt; s.find('z') == std::string::npos;</code>","a":"It does not compile as intended: <code>&lt;&lt;</code> binds tighter than <code>==</code>, so it tries to compare the stream with <code>npos</code>. Parenthesise: <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; (s.find('z') == std::string::npos);</code> prints 1."},{"id":"compiletime-0","topic":"compiletime","q":"What does SFINAE stand for and why does it exist?","a":"Substitution Failure Is Not An Error: an invalid substitution while deducing a template removes that candidate from overload resolution instead of causing a hard error. It is what makes <code>enable_if</code> overload selection work."},{"id":"compiletime-1","topic":"compiletime","q":"<code>std::is_same_v&lt;int, const int&gt;</code>?","a":"False. They are different types. <code>const</code> and references are part of the type, which is why traits like <code>std::decay_t</code> and <code>std::remove_cvref_t</code> exist."},{"id":"compiletime-2","topic":"compiletime","q":"<code>constexpr</code> function versus <code>consteval</code> function?","a":"<code>constexpr</code> may run at compile time or run time. <code>consteval</code> must run at compile time and is a compile error otherwise."},{"id":"compiletime-3","topic":"compiletime","q":"Why is <code>if constexpr</code> better than <code>if</code> in a template?","a":"The discarded branch is not instantiated, so code that is invalid for some <code>T</code> does not break the build for that <code>T</code>."},{"id":"compiletime-4","topic":"compiletime","q":"How do you avoid writing the factorial struct by hand today?","a":"Write <code>constexpr int factorial(int n)</code> and use it in a <code>constexpr</code> context. It is shorter, readable and can also run at run time."},{"id":"rules-0","topic":"rules","q":"What is MISRA, and how does AUTOSAR C++14 relate to it?","a":"MISRA is a family of coding guidelines for safe use of C and C++ in critical systems. AUTOSAR C++14 is a guideline for C++14 in safety-related environments, based on MISRA C++:2008 and updated for modern C++."},{"id":"rules-1","topic":"rules","q":"Why would a safety standard forbid recursion?","a":"Stack depth cannot be bounded statically, so overflow is possible; an iterative version with a bounded loop can be analysed."},{"id":"rules-2","topic":"rules","q":"Why avoid dynamic allocation in embedded real-time code?","a":"Allocation time is unpredictable, the heap can fragment, and allocation can fail at run time. Allocate once at start-up or use fixed-size pools."},{"id":"rules-3","topic":"rules","q":"What is the difference between a coding guideline and a code style?","a":"Guidelines restrict how language features are used to avoid errors. Style fixes the appearance of the code: indentation, brace placement, naming."},{"id":"rules-4","topic":"rules","q":"You must break a rule. What do you do?","a":"Record a justified deviation through the project's process (comment plus tool suppression referencing it), get it reviewed, and keep it as narrow as possible."},{"id":"wire-0","topic":"wire","q":"Why not cast the receive buffer to a struct pointer?","a":"<div><p>Four problems at once: it violates strict aliasing (undefined behavior), the buffer address may not be aligned for the struct, struct padding may not match the wire layout, and the byte order may differ. Check the length, then copy each field out with <code>memcpy</code> or assemble it with shifts.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-1","topic":"wire","q":"What is struct padding and how do you reduce it?","a":"<div><p>The compiler inserts unused bytes so each member sits at a multiple of its alignment, and pads the end so arrays stay aligned. <code>sizeof</code> can exceed the sum of the members. Order members from largest to smallest alignment; use <code>static_assert(sizeof(...))</code> to guard the size.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-2","topic":"wire","q":"What does <code>#pragma pack(1)</code> do, and what does it cost?","a":"<div><p>It removes padding so the struct matches a wire layout. The cost: members may be unaligned, which is slower or faults on some CPUs, and references or pointers to them are unsafe. Prefer reading fields with <code>memcpy</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-3","topic":"wire","q":"What is endianness? Which does the network use?","a":"<div><p>The order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Little-endian stores the least significant byte first (x86, ARM), big-endian the most significant first. Network byte order is big-endian, though a given exchange protocol may differ, so check the spec. Convert at the boundary.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-4","topic":"wire","q":"How do you read a big-endian 32-bit value portably?","a":"<div><p>Assemble it from the bytes with shifts, widening each byte to <code>uint32_t</code> first: <code>(uint32_t{p[0]} &lt;&lt; 24) | (uint32_t{p[1]} &lt;&lt; 16) | (uint32_t{p[2]} &lt;&lt; 8) | uint32_t{p[3]}</code>. It works on any CPU.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-5","topic":"wire","q":"What is strict aliasing?","a":"<div><p>The rule that an object may only be accessed through an lvalue of its own type (or a few related types); the compiler assumes this when optimizing. Exceptions: <code>char</code>, <code>unsigned char</code> and <code>std::byte</code> may alias anything. Use <code>memcpy</code> or <code>std::bit_cast</code> to reinterpret bytes.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-6","topic":"wire","q":"<code>memcpy</code>, <code>bit_cast</code> or a union for type punning?","a":"<div><p><code>memcpy</code> and <code>std::bit_cast</code> (C++20) are well defined and compile to a plain load. Reading a different union member than was written is undefined behavior in C++, even though GCC allows it as an extension.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-7","topic":"wire","q":"How do you set, clear and test a bit, and check for a power of two?","a":"<div><p>Set <code>x | (1u &lt;&lt; n)</code>; clear <code>x &amp; ~(1u &lt;&lt; n)</code>; test <code>(x &gt;&gt; n) &amp; 1</code>. Power of two: <code>x != 0 &amp;&amp; (x &amp; (x - 1)) == 0</code>. Use unsigned types and keep <code>n</code> below the width.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-8","topic":"wire","q":"Why should prices not be <code>double</code>?","a":"<div><p>Binary floating point cannot represent most decimals exactly (<code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code>), so sums and comparisons drift. Store an integer count of ticks (a scaled <code>int64_t</code>): exact, deterministic, and comparable with <code>==</code>. Parse text straight into ticks and watch for overflow when multiplying by quantity.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-9","topic":"wire","q":"Why <code>std::from_chars</code> in a parser?","a":"<div><p>It converts text to numbers without allocating, throwing or consulting the locale, and reports errors through a return value plus the position where it stopped. <code>stoi</code> throws and needs a <code>std::string</code>; <code>stringstream</code> is slow.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-10","topic":"wire","q":"How do you detect a gap in sequence numbers, and handle wrap-around?","a":"<div><p>Compare the received number with the expected one: equal is in order, higher is a gap (lost messages: buffer and request a retransmission or snapshot), lower is a duplicate. For wrap-around compute <code>static_cast&lt;int32_t&gt;(received - expected)</code> and look at its sign.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-0","topic":"latency","q":"Why does tail latency matter more than the average in a feed handler?","a":"<div><p>The rare slow case (p99.9) is what produces a stale book or a missed trade, and the average hides it: a mean of 281 ns can coexist with a p99.9 of 80 microseconds. Measure and report percentiles, and remove sources of unpredictable delay.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-1","topic":"latency","q":"Why avoid <code>new</code> on the hot path, and what do you do instead?","a":"<div><p>The allocator can take a lock, walk free lists, fragment and call the kernel, so its cost is unpredictable. Allocate at startup: <code>reserve</code> containers, use an object pool or an arena (<code>std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource</code>), and use fixed buffers instead of strings and vectors for small data.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-2","topic":"latency","q":"How does an object pool work?","a":"<div><p>A fixed array of slots, plus a free list threaded through the free slots. Allocation pops the head of the list and constructs the object there with placement new; deallocation destroys it and pushes the slot back. Both are a few instructions with no heap, and when the pool is empty the caller decides what to do.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-3","topic":"latency","q":"Array of structs versus struct of arrays?","a":"<div><p>AoS keeps one record's fields together, good when you use most fields. SoA keeps each field in its own array, good when a loop uses one or two fields: it streams only the bytes it needs, so each cache line is fully useful. Same results, less memory traffic.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-4","topic":"latency","q":"What is false sharing and how do you fix it?","a":"<div><p>Two threads writing different variables on the same 64-byte cache line force the line to bounce between cores. Give each variable its own line with <code>alignas(64)</code> or padding, as for the head and tail indices of an SPSC queue.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-5","topic":"latency","q":"Why is a vector or array usually faster than <code>std::map</code> or <code>std::list</code>?","a":"<div><p>Contiguous memory is cache-friendly and prefetchable; node-based containers chase pointers across the heap and allocate per node. Big-O hides these constants, especially for small and medium sizes.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-6","topic":"latency","q":"How would you structure an order book?","a":"<div><p>For a bounded price window, an array of quantities indexed by price tick: O(1) updates, no allocation, with the best price tracked and rescanned when its level empties. A hash map from order id to its price and quantity handles cancels. A <code>std::map</code> is simpler but allocates a node per level.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-7","topic":"latency","q":"Why is an SPSC ring capacity a power of two?","a":"<div><p>The wrap-around becomes <code>counter &amp; (N - 1)</code> instead of a modulo, one cheap instruction, and the head and tail counters can simply keep counting up while <code>tail - head</code> is the size.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-8","topic":"latency","q":"What do you do when a queue between threads is full?","a":"<div><p>Never let it grow without bound. Choose a policy: block (back-pressure), drop the newest, drop the oldest, or conflate to the latest per instrument, count what was dropped, and recover from the gap if needed. A market-data reader normally cannot block.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-9","topic":"latency","q":"Why avoid exceptions, virtual calls, locks and logging on the hot path?","a":"<div><p>Each adds cost that is large or unpredictable: a throw costs microseconds, a virtual call blocks inlining and is an indirect branch, a mutex can wait and context-switch, and logging means system calls and formatting. Use error codes, templates or CRTP, single-writer data, and an asynchronous logger fed by a queue.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-10","topic":"latency","q":"Busy-polling versus blocking, and why pin threads?","a":"<div><p>Blocking saves CPU but a wake-up costs microseconds; busy-polling reacts almost immediately but burns a whole core, so only latency-critical threads spin. Pinning a thread to a core stops migration, which keeps its caches warm and its timing stable.</p></div>"}];
